{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"CORRECTION — my prev_snapshot claim was WRONG, and the reviewer caught it. Retracting it here rather\nthan leaving a plausible-sounding falsehood in the plan.\n\nI wrote that a leftover production prev_snapshot.json 'drives the deletion path across hundreds of\nlocal tickets'. Traced properly: prev_snapshot is a BASELINE store for ALREADY-BOUND tickets.\nseed_baselines_from_snapshot (binding_store.py:377) only acts where 'jira_key in prev_snapshot', and\n_local_matches_prev uses it for FIELD-CHANGE detection. It is not a remote inventory and it does not\ndrive deletion — deletion detection runs off the absence PROBE on bound keys.\n\nAnd since the scrub removes bindings.json, its entries would match nothing anyway.\n\nI reasoned from a filename instead of tracing the code, then stated it as fact — twice in one turn\n(the other was attributing the local harness failures to resource contention when the README documents\narm64 emulation).\n\nTHE SCRUB REQUIREMENT STANDS, on a correct basis: bindings.json and bindings-retired.json map local\ntickets to REAL production Jira keys and must go. prev_snapshot.json still goes too — it contains real\nproduction Jira field data, and a surviving baseline would cause spurious field-change detection if any\nbinding survived the scrub. What changes is the JUSTIFICATION, not the action. The '.bak-retarget'\ntrap and the enumerate-don't-hardcode rule are unaffected.\n\nALSO FROM THIS ROUND — a new SHIPPED defect, filed as [rebar:9263-b404-cb6c-463f]:\nget_issuelinks_map and get_comment_map call search_issues(f'project = {key}') with NO paging, taking\nthe default max_results=50. Beyond 50 issues, links and comments are silently invisible. That is the\nSAME defect class as get_parent_map, which I fixed in change 1105 — and when I fixed it I patched that\none method instead of sweeping every search_issues caller. Two siblings left behind. On finding a\ndefect class, audit for siblings before closing.\n\nSTILL TO FIX IN THIS PLAN from round 3: the outbound mutation set is only 'the mirror set' and must be\nenumerated; 'mass deletion' needs a quantitative threshold; and 'edit comment' must be REMOVED — the\nreconciler has no edit-comment mutation type and the DC transport has no update_comment, so I\nspecified a capability that does not exist."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785513708298533001,"uuid":"0346400e-4845-46d7-a790-0994f5bf85c0"}